front_page"],"author":"klaussilveira","children":[50218386,50218449,50218559,50218787,50219362,50219999],"created_at":"2026-10-22T10:55:22Z","created_at_i":1792666522,"num_comments":14,"objectID":"50193677","points":126,"story_id":50193677,"title":"The Quake Fan who built the map editor everybody uses","updated_at":"2026-10-24T11:28:57Z","url":"https://funcarena.com/articles/the-quake-fan-who-built-the-map-editor-everybody-uses"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"westsmith"},"title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"A practical guide to AI image generation, from diffusion to agents"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://danielball.com/notes/ai_image_generation.html"}},"_tags":["story","author_westsmith","story_50171379","front_page"],"author":"westsmith","children":[50171387,50172003,50183585,50218854,50218867,50218895,50218954,50219021,50219325,50219568,50220225],"created_at":"2026-10-20T19:22:39Z","created_at_i":1792524159,"num_comments":30,"objectID":"50171379","points":107,"story_id":50171379,"title":"A practical guide to AI image generation, from diffusion to agents","updated_at":"2026-10-24T11:25:42Z","url":"https://danielball.com/notes/ai_image_generation.html"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"eiopa"},"story_text":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Hey everyone,<p>Inspired by Herdr, I've built an agent multiplexer that I've been using as my daily driver for a few weeks now.<p>I think I improved on the concept in a few ways, focusing on the ergonomics I always wished I had:<p>I show you what the agents are up to, along with a running feed of their activity.\nThere's also a simple shortcut (F1) to go to the next unread agent.<p>I use multiple accounts, so I've added unified launcher for Codex/Claude Code/Pi.\nTheres some careful ergonomics there: fast search and resume over sessions, inline usage tracking etc.<p>Many small quality of life improvements to the terminal experience:\n- Links can be copied with a click, so no need to drag-select over many lines\n- cmd+v just pastes images (even on SSH)\n- images have a thumbnail preview.\n- Built-in CPU/Memory/GPU usage tracking, broken down by workspace<p>I assume you have access to cheap inference (Luna/Haiku/etc).\nIt just reuses the agents to organize things for you: what is this http link? what is this worktree about? what is each agent doing? \nIt's small but it made my environment feel more cozy.<p>On a personal note, I've been on a frustrating sick leave for a while now.\nI created Corgi to challenge myself, and see if I can go back.\nThe answer is more mixed than I had hoped, but I am happy with the result and thankful for the experience.<p>I hope you like it! a few friends &amp; myself have been using it for a few weeks and it's been pretty nice.\nThere's still a lot to improve and some rough edges, so feedback would be greatly appreciated.<p>Thank you :)"},"title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Show HN: Corgi, a helpful {agent, terminal} multiplexer"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://corgi.computer/"}},"_tags":["story","author_eiopa","story_50214373","show_hn","front_page"],"author":"eiopa","children":[50217001,50217236,50217652,50217777,50217919,50218291,50218331,50218790,50219039,50219094,50219172,50219250,50219252,50219706,50219839,50219921],"created_at":"2026-10-23T19:46:38Z","created_at_i":1792784798,"num_comments":24,"objectID":"50214373","points":92,"story_id":50214373,"story_text":"Hey everyone,<p>Inspired by Herdr, I&#x27;ve built an agent multiplexer that I&#x27;ve been using as my daily driver for a few weeks now.<p>I think I improved on the concept in a few ways, focusing on the ergonomics I always wished I had:<p>I show you what the agents are up to, along with a running feed of their activity.\nThere&#x27;s also a simple shortcut (F1) to go to the next unread agent.<p>I use multiple accounts, so I&#x27;ve added unified launcher for Codex&#x2F;Claude Code&#x2F;Pi.\nTheres some careful ergonomics there: fast search and resume over sessions, inline usage tracking etc.<p>Many small quality of life improvements to the terminal experience:\n- Links can be copied with a click, so no need to drag-select over many lines\n- cmd+v just pastes images (even on SSH)\n- images have a thumbnail preview.\n- Built-in CPU&#x2F;Memory&#x2F;GPU usage tracking, broken down by workspace<p>I assume you have access to cheap inference (Luna&#x2F;Haiku&#x2F;etc).\nIt just reuses the agents to organize things for you: what is this http link? what is this worktree about? what is each agent doing? \nIt&#x27;s small but it made my environment feel more cozy.<p>On a personal note, I&#x27;ve been on a frustrating sick leave for a while now.\nI created Corgi to challenge myself, and see if I can go back.\nThe answer is more mixed than I had hoped, but I am happy with the result and thankful for the experience.<p>I hope you like it! a few friends &amp; myself have been using it for a few weeks and it&#x27;s been pretty nice.\nThere&#x27;s still a lot to improve and some rough edges, so feedback would be greatly appreciated.<p>Thank you :)","title":"Show HN: Corgi, a helpful {agent, terminal} multiplexer","updated_at":"2026-10-24T11:32:42Z","url":"https://corgi.computer/"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"martz"},"title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"C vs. C#: racing VLC's Gaussian blur"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://martinfinkel.com/blog/gaussian-blur-c-vs-csharp/"}},"_tags":["story","author_martz","story_50176544","front_page"],"author":"martz","children":[50218226,50218375,50218464,50218669,50218820,50218830,50219023,50219231],"created_at":"2026-10-21T05:48:12Z","created_at_i":1792561692,"num_comments":23,"objectID":"50176544","points":89,"story_id":50176544,"title":